The review result is **FINDINGS**: I found no defects in movement, playability or the audio pipeline. It can't be PASS because some checks still need a person or other browsers. The report is in `artifacts/logs/reviewer_gameplay.aln28c/report.md`.

- **Same build as before:** the source and the built game are unchanged since the previous gameplay review and the other roles' latest reports. I didn't rebuild.
- **What I ran:** the 9 gameplay test scripts from the previous review, unchanged, against my own local server in headless Chromium. All 9 completed without failures, and the two recorded notes are expected Doom behaviour.
  - **Movement:** after you let go of forward, the player slides and stops after about 47 tics, as Doom's normal friction should. Turning stops dead when the key is released. Moving diagonally and in arcs works.
  - **Focus loss:** when the window loses focus, held turning and forward movement stop, and the keys work again afterwards.
  - **Fire, use and doors:** firing uses ammo and plays a sound, and holding fire repeats until release. A closed door blocks the player, Use opens it, and the player can walk through.
  - **Rest of the game:** pause, damage, death and respawn work. The level exit leads to the next level, E1M2, and the player can move there.
  - **Timing and audio:** the game runs at the correct 35 tics per second. All 19 requested sound effects played, and music started 3 of 3 times with no errors.
- **Screenshots:** I looked at the open door and the E1M2 level; both draw correctly.

**Still unverified:**
- Whether the sound effects and music sound right when you listen to them. The tests only prove the audio isn't silent.
- How the game behaves in a genuinely hidden or minimized tab.
- Firefox, Safari, high-resolution screens, the end of the episode, and a full walkthrough without the no-clipping cheat.

**Limits of this review:**
- I didn't re-read the test scripts' logic; I only confirmed they are identical to the previous run's, so any mistake in them carries over.
- Testing was headless Chromium only, on this Mac.
- Nothing is assigned to a coder.

I stopped the server I started.

**Next steps:** a person should check the audio by ear and try a hidden tab, using the protocol in `tests/README.md`. The tester still has the carried-over work of turning these gameplay checks into protected acceptance checks.